 Here we have $n=2$ and the matrix is 
\[\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix}  -1 & 2 \\  3 i & 4 \end{pmatrix}.\] The entry in the bottom left position is not an integer, so we will have to rationalize the denominator.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  7 & -2  \\  6 &  13 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^4&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  85 & -18  \\  30 &  181 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^4=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 &   6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^3&=\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  -7 & 14  \\  81 & 124 \end{pmatrix} \\
-3\mathbf{A}&=\begin{pmatrix}  3 & -6  \\  -9 & -12 \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Consider finding a linear combination of $-\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}^2$ that is as close as possible to minimizing the $F_1$ entry of $-\mathbf{A}$.  There are 4 coefficients that are integers:

\begin{align*}
-3\mathbf{A}-13\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  46 & -34  \\  -165 &-473  \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Suppose instead that $-\mathbf{A}$ is bunched up by a smaller amount, with $t$ coefficients, so $t(2\mathbf{A}+t\mathbf{A}^2)=-\mathbf{A}.$  Then
\[t\mathbf{A}^3=(2t+1)\mathbf{A}+(t^0)\mathbf{A}^4\]because these are both coefficient matrices.  Hence, 
\[(2t+1)6+(t^2)181=-1.\]
One is sure that there are 13, 3, 4, and a limit as $t^2$ goes to infinity, which occurs only when the solution is in the intersection of $(-3\mathbf{A}-13\mathbf{A}^2=-\mathbf{A})$ and $-\mathbf{A}+t(2\mathbf{A}+t\mathbf{A}^2)=0,$ so that,
\[(2t+1)6+(t^2)181=1\].
The equation is 
\[181t^2+12t-12=0.\]  Solving, 
\[t=-\frac{2+\sqrt{345}}{305}\] is the solution since $0\leq t \leq 1$. Then,
\[p=t^2\mathbf{A}^4=-\frac{124+48\sqrt{345}}{93025}\]
and
\[q=-t\mathbf{A}^3=\frac{1+14\sqrt{345}}{93025}.\] 
\end{align*}

