So let's start from scratch. Since $\log_{4}3=x$, then $4^{x}=3$. Also, $\log_{2}27=kx$. Using the change of base formula, we get $k=\frac{\log_{4}27}{\log_{4}4}$. Since $\log_{4}4=1$, we can simplify this to $k=\log_{4}27$]</think> $$\begin{aligned}

&\phantom{=}\log_{4}27

~\\=&\frac{\log_{2}27}{\log_{2}4}&\text*{\quad{We write the logarithm as a quotient using the change of base formula}}

~\\=&\frac{kx}{\log_{2}4}&\text*{\quad{We write $\log_{4}3$ as $x$.You could also solve the problem without changing base.}} ,

\end{aligned}$$

and, since $\log_{2}4=2$, we obtain $k=\frac{1}{2}\log_{2}27$. Now, we want $k$ in terms of $x$. Since $\log_2 3 = \log_2 \left(3^1\right) = \log_2 \left(3^{\frac1{\log_3 4}}\right) = \frac1{\log_3 4} \log_2 3 = \frac1{\log_3 4}$, we have $\log_2 27 = \log_2 3^3 = \frac3{\log_3 4} = \frac3{\log_4 3^{\log_4 3}} = \frac3{(\log_4 3)^2} = \frac3{x^2}$. Therefore, $k = \frac{1}{2}\cdot \frac3{x^2}$.

